# PromptLineAI – Cursor Rules (Extension POC)

## 1) Scope & Change Control
- Do not add features beyond the explicit request.
- Do not refactor unrelated code or “clean up” without asking.
- Keep diffs minimal and targeted.
- Do not change the event schema unless explicitly requested. Propose changes first.

## 2) Dependencies & Upgrades
- Do not add new dependencies unless absolutely necessary.
- Prefer built-in Web APIs and small utilities over libraries.
- Do not upgrade Node, TypeScript, esbuild, or any dependency without approval.
- If a dependency is required, justify it briefly.
- Do not regenerate `pnpm-lock.yaml` unless dependencies change.

## 3) Chrome Extension Constraints
- Must target **Manifest V3** only.
- Chromium browsers only (Chrome first). No Firefox/Safari work unless requested.
- Request the minimum permissions required. Explain any new permission.
- Avoid fragile DOM selectors; use MutationObserver and fallback strategies.
- Never capture or store prompt content or file content. Metadata only.

## 4) Security & Privacy
- Never store secrets in the extension (no API keys, tokens, or passwords).
- Treat all extension code as public and inspectable.
- Do not log sensitive data (no prompt text, no file contents).
- Default to `DRY_RUN=true`. Do not send data externally by default.
- All network requests must be explicit and configurable.

## 5) Code Quality
- Use strict TypeScript. Avoid `any`.
- Keep modules small and single-purpose.
- Use clear, descriptive naming. Avoid clever abstractions.
- All site-specific logic must live in `src/adapters/<tool>.ts`.
- Core pipeline must remain: `content → background → queue → sender`.
- Handle errors explicitly. Do not swallow errors.

## 6) Data Integrity & Robustness
- Generate a UUID per event.
- Persist the event queue in `chrome.storage.local`.
- Implement retry with backoff; avoid infinite loops.
- Enforce a max queue size and drop oldest events if needed (log once).
- Batch sends (max 50 events). Never send per-event requests.

## 7) Performance
- Do not degrade host page performance.
- Use MutationObserver carefully; debounce processing and disconnect when stable.
- Avoid polling loops in content scripts.
- Keep service worker memory usage low; rely on storage.

## 8) Formatting
- Follow consistent formatting:
  - 2-space indentation
  - single quotes where reasonable
  - trailing commas where valid
  - no semicolons unless required
- Keep line length ~100 characters.
- Do not add heavy linting/formatting tooling unless requested.

## 9) Build & Packaging (esbuild)
- Build outputs must go to `/dist`.
- Do not change the build system (esbuild) unless requested.
- Ensure `manifest.json` and static assets are copied to `/dist`.
- No source maps in production builds unless requested.
- Keep build scripts simple and deterministic.

## 10) Logging & Debugging
- Prefix logs with `[PromptLineAI]`.
- Gate verbose logs behind a `DEBUG` flag.
- Background logs may include queue size and flush results only.
- Content script logs should confirm adapter init and event detection, not spam.

## 11) Popup UI (Optional)
- Popup is minimal and optional.
- No additional permissions for popup.
- Read-only view of recent events from storage.
- No frameworks unless explicitly requested.

## 12) Testing (POC-Level)
- No heavy test frameworks required.
- If tests exist, limit to core utilities (queue, validation).
- Prefer runtime validation and defensive coding.

## 13) Documentation
- Update README only when behavior or setup changes.
- README must clearly state:
  - supported domains
  - events collected
  - what is NOT collected
  - how to load unpacked extension
  - where to see logs

## Stop-and-Ask Triggers
If any of the following are needed, stop and ask before implementing:
- New permissions
- Capturing any content beyond metadata
- Adding dependencies
- Changing the event schema
- Adding auth or credential handling
- Expanding to additional AI tools

